{% comment %}
  robots.txt

  Shopify's own default rules are emitted verbatim via `robots.default_groups`. They
  already cover the paths that must never be crawled — /admin, /cart, /checkout,
  /account, /orders, sorted collection URLs, theme-preview parameters — and they already
  publish the sitemap. Rewriting those by hand is how stores accidentally deindex
  themselves, so they are passed through untouched.

  Three things are layered on top:

    1. An explicit allow for the major AI and answer-engine crawlers. They are not
       blocked by default, but stating it means a future change to Shopify's defaults
       cannot quietly cut the store out of AI search results.
    2. A pointer to llms.txt, for the assistants that look for one.
    3. A crawl-delay for the bandwidth-heavy SEO-tool bots, which crawl aggressively
       and return nothing.

  Deliberately NOT blocked here: faceted collection URLs (`?filter.*`). Those are handled
  with `noindex, follow` plus a canonical back to the clean collection in
  `snippets/meta-tags.liquid`. Blocking them in robots.txt would stop Google reading that
  noindex, so any already-indexed filter URLs could never drop out.
{% endcomment %}
{% for group in robots.default_groups %}
  {{- group.user_agent }}

  {% for rule in group.rules %}{{ rule }}
  {% endfor %}

  {%- if group.sitemap != blank %}
    {{ group.sitemap }}
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
